AArch64 QEMU TCG performance baseline

Manual local measurements only. Do not add these workloads to QEMU CI or use
their timings as CI thresholds. Cross-architecture TCG and shared runner CPU
availability make GitHub performance numbers unsuitable for this comparison.
The runners refuse execution when CI=true or GITHUB_ACTIONS=true.
For physical Pi 5 storage/network measurements, use tests/hardware/storage
and tests/hardware/network. Those are separate manual fixtures and are not
built or packaged by default; their results are not comparable to TCG timings.

This fixture compares a Native HypeR process with a direct Linux process on
the same QEMU machine. It measures application-visible performance, including
the HypeR I/O VM storage path. It is not a nested business VM comparison or a
measurement of physical DRAM or SSD bandwidth.

Prerequisites

Use the repository's Clang/LLVM and pinned Rust toolchains. Install QEMU,
dosfstools and mtools, assemble/cache the Native SDK and application dependencies,
and fetch the normal AArch64 Alpine guest assets and the QEMU I/O appliance.
Supply a complete production AArch64 kernel configuration. The preparation
script rebuilds the normal image and applications without test/audit features;
it copies the configuration rather than modifying kernel/.config.

From the repository root:

  python3 -B tests/performance/prepare.py \
    --output target/performance-baseline/fixture \
    --io-package target/io-vm/packages/PACKAGE_DIGEST \
    --config /absolute/path/to/aarch64.config

  python3 -B tests/performance/run.py \
    --fixture target/performance-baseline/fixture \
    --output target/performance-baseline/results

Both output directories must be new. The runner requires local Unix socket
access for QMP. Keep paths short enough for macOS Unix sockets. Do not run
compilers or other benchmarks concurrently. For a preliminary smoke run, append
--order hyper linux; the default is hyper/linux/linux/hyper/hyper/linux.

To measure a kernel change against the identical workloads, initramfs and disk,
keep the fixture immutable and pass --hyper-image path/to/new/hyper.img. The
runner records the override's path and SHA-256. Build a production image with
the fixture's kernel.config; do not use stack-audit or profiling features.

Controlled configuration

QEMU virt-11.1, virtualization enabled, GICv3, max CPU, explicit multi-thread TCG,
4 CPUs and 1 GiB RAM. No network or instruction-count clock. Both systems use
the same virtio-scsi/raw disk/cache=writeback configuration. Each boot receives
a fresh copy of the same GPT/FAT32 image, using the production board's volume
size and formatter defaults. The runner reads each copy before boot, so host
cache state is warm. Linux mounts FAT32 with noatime. HypeR keeps its ordinary
services and its 128 MiB, one-vCPU I/O VM, all within the outer resource budget;
there are no business VMs and no added CPU affinity policy.

The memory workload is compiled once at -O2 -march=armv8-a and the same object
is linked into both executables. Platform adapters provide clocks and file I/O:
the installed HypeR SDK std bridge and direct AArch64 Linux syscalls. This avoids
comparing different libc memcpy implementations. Compiler barriers retain the
loops, warm-up occurs before timing, and read/copy results are checked.

Measurements

* memory_copy / memory_read: 8 MiB resident arrays, 256 passes per sample,
  three samples. Copy counts payload bytes once, not read-plus-write traffic.
  Allocation, page fault, NUMA and multi-thread scaling are not measured.
* file_read_first: first application data read after boot, 16 MiB, 128 KiB
  requests. Guest filesystem metadata may already be warm from mounting.
* file_read_repeat: two immediate repeats, reported separately from first
  reads to distinguish guest cache reuse from the initial backend traversal.
* file_random_read_4k: 512 deterministic requests per sample, three samples,
  after sequential reading, one application request outstanding.
* file_grow_sync: write a newly created file to 16 MiB and fsync once. Open and
  create are outside the interval; allocation during growth is included.
* file_overwrite_sync: overwrite the allocated file twice, fsync per pass.
* file_random_write_sync_4k: 64 deterministic overwrites per sample, fsync
  after each request, three samples. No O_DIRECT is used in either OS.
* file_random_read_hot_4k: 512 deterministic aligned requests per sample over
  the first 256 KiB of seed.bin, three samples. The complete 64-page working
  set is read and checked before each sample, outside timing. Every returned
  byte is checked afterward using the existing resident memory buffer. This
  isolates a small working set; the 16 MiB scan/random cases exercise a
  larger one. HypeR cache capacity now grows with available managed RAM. The hot case
  runs after all existing cases and readback checks, preserving their order
  and data. Both systems run the same code; run.py reports this case in IOPS.

The native kernel may batch device requests internally; QD1 describes the
application, not every layer below it. Guest monotonic clocks measure each
interval; a host monotonic clock also records the complete workload. No console
output or data verification occurs within timed intervals. Full seed checks
and write readback run outside them. After each QEMU exits, mtools verifies the
complete written file through the host image, independently of guest caches.
This is a readback check, not a power-failure durability proof.

Results and limits

Build identities, artifact SHA-256 hashes, exact QEMU commands, host metadata,
serial logs, nanosecond samples, CSV data and QMP block counters are preserved.
The summary takes the median within each boot, then the median across boots;
min/max describe boot medians. Temporary per-run disks are removed after
successful readback; failed-run disks remain for diagnosis. QEMU processes are
owned by the runner and terminated on success and failure.

QMP counters cover the entire workload, including untimed verification reads;
the added hot-case warmup also contributes to them. Do not describe these
counters as the traffic of a single 16 MiB sample. Short Linux
cached reads can be only a few milliseconds: treat their precise throughput
as exploratory. Three boots are an initial baseline, not confidence intervals.
TCG includes translation and software MMU overhead and does not model the host
CPU's memory hierarchy as guest hardware. Results must not be extrapolated to
bare-metal HypeR or hardware-assisted virtualization. Raw block performance,
ext4, larger-than-memory datasets, concurrent I/O and long-duration tail
latency require separate workloads.

Cold and repeated VM startup

vm-start.py measures HypeR startup through an ordinary Alpine VM loaded from
the board FAT volume. Prepare a disposable board-image fixture from boards/qemu.json,
set Alpine autostart=true, and keep board.json, bootstrap.cpio and base.img in
its directory. Use make board-image with explicit BOARD_CONFIG, BOARD_OUTPUT,
BOARD_IMAGE and CONFIG_FILE paths and the cached IO_VM_PACKAGE. Save its normal
kernel image alongside it before changing source or rebuilding.

  python3 -B tests/performance/vm-start.py \
    --fixture target/local-startup/fixture \
    --image target/local-startup/hyper.img \
    --output target/local-startup/results

Each of the default three boots starts fresh HypeR and Linux guests, warms only
the host image cache, and uses QEMU snapshot writes that are discarded at exit.
The script reports runtime entry to vCPU start submission, host process start
to that submission, and host start to a working Alpine shell. The last interval
includes console attachment and guest boot; it is not pure file-read latency.
It verifies a guest shell command before cleaning up its owned QEMU process.
For an application change, pass --initramfs with the new production archive;
keep the board and initial disk identical. --boots 1 is useful for diagnosis.
Use --starts 3 to follow the initial autostart with two vmm stop/start alpine
cycles in the same HypeR boot. Each cycle verifies the guest shell and the final
cycle stops the VM. Results retain cold-start fields and report each restart
separately; restart wall time starts immediately before the vmm start command.
The manager retains its image handle between cycles, so these measurements
exercise ITB cache reuse, not final-handle-close identity retention. The FAT
lifecycle tests cover that separate contract.
The existing startup-profile runtime feature can explain stages in a separate
archive. Its per-payload read/write durations are recorded under starts[].payloads;
final timing comparisons must use ordinary production applications.
This startup runner is also local/manual only, with no CI performance gates.
